<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >Actually, that's not true.  Squaw comes from the Massachusett word <i>squa</i> which means "female, younger woman".  The misconception that squaw means "vagina" (or something more vulgar) was actually perpetuated on Oprah (go figure) a few years ago.  The misconception originally comes from a book called <i>Literature of the American Indian</i> from 1973, which said that squaw was derived from the Mohawk word, <i>ojiskwa</i> which DOES mean "vagina".  But the last syllable of <i>ojiskwa</i> is not pronounced the same as the word <i>squa</i>.  The visual similarity is just a coincidince.<br>
<br>
So, although it may be unfavorable to use the term, it's not necessarily any worse than the words "papoose", "sachem", "skunk", "opossum", and "raccoon" which were also derived from Massachusett and other Algonquian languages.  Even if the white man has sometimes used it in a derogatory way, it's not a derogatory word in and of itself.<br>
<br>
<br>
Oh, and to answer DP..."Kangaroo" means "I don't understand."  If this seems weird, consider this exchange:<br>
<br>
<br>
White guy (pointing at a "kangaroo"):  What the givl is that thing?!<br>
Aboriginal:  Kangaroo.  (Translation: Huh?)<br>
<br>
Terry Pratchett makes fun of this fact in one of his books by saying that the word for a particular animal is a native word meaning, "The end of your finger, you idiot" (or something like that)<br>
<br>
<br>
